<chapter xml:id="ch-intro-symbolic">
  <title>Introduction to Symbolic Calculations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Symbolic computation is a branch of computer science and mathematics that focuses on manipulating mathematical
      expressions in a symbolic form, rather than performing numerical calculations.
      This allows for exact solutions to problems, as well as the ability to manipulate and simplify expressions
      algebraically.
    </p>

    <p>
      To get started with this chapter, we will need to load the <c>Symbolics</c> package, so ensure that you have it
      installed and ready to use.
      There are related packages that we will see later as well.
      Although the <url href="https://docs.sciml.ai/Symbolics/stable/">Symbolics.jl documentation</url> is a great
      resource, this chapter aims to provide a more hands-on introduction to symbolic calculations.
    </p>

    <p>
      Of course, we need to load the <c>Symbolics</c> package and we will also using the <c>Latexify</c> package as
      well, which will help us display symbolic expressions in a more readable format.
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        using Symbolics, Latexify
      </code>
    </program>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-symbolic-variables">
    <title>Symbolic Variables and Expressions</title>

    <p>
      To do any symbolic calculations, we first need to define symbolic variables.
      In Symbolics.jl, this is done using the <c>@variables</c> macro.
      For example, to define two symbolic variables <c>x</c> and <c>y</c>, we would write:
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        @variables x y t
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      This creates symbolic variables which differ from regular Julia variables in that they can be used in symbolic
      expressions and manipulated algebraically, instead of julia variables, which store Julia numbers and objects.
    </p>

    <p>
      With these symbolic variables, we can create symbolic expressions.
      For example, we can define a symbolic expression for a polynomial:
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        p = 3x^3 - x
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      The output of this is <m>3x^3-x</m> and will be formatting as mathematics if you have loaded the <c>Latexify</c>
      package.
    </p>

    <p>
      The julia variable <c>p</c> is an expression in that if you evaluate <c>typeof(p)</c> the result is <c>Num</c>,
      which is the type of all Symbolic expressions using the <c>Symbolics</c> package.
      Also, this isn't a function (either a julia or mathematical), but we can create those and will see that below.
    </p>

    <p>
      Additionally, you may want an exact value of a number.
      If we enter <c>sqrt(2)</c> then the floating point approximation to <m>\sqrt{2}</m> appears.
      If we want a number to not be automatically applied to a function, you can wrap the <c>Num</c> function around it
      which will give you a symbolic number.
      For example,
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        sqrt(Num(2))
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      will return <m>\sqrt{2}</m> in a symbolic sense.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-symbolic-substitution">
    <title>Substitution</title>

    <p>
      Substitution, simplifying and expanding all standard operations that people do with expression when manipulating
      mathematical expressions and this can be done with symbolic expressions.
    </p>

    <p>
      We'll first examine substitution, which replaces an expression with another expression.
      If we enter:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        substitute(p, x =&gt; -2)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and this returns <c>-22</c> and if you check, this is a <c>Num</c>, not an <c>Int</c>.
      We will learn below how to convert this to a julia <c>Int</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The <c>substitute</c> command is more powerful when substituting expressions for other expressions.
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        substitute(p, x=&gt; y^2-t)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      then the result is <m>t-y^2+3(-t+y^2)^3</m>.
      Simply, everywhere in <c>p</c> with an <c>x</c>, it is replaced with <m>y^2-t</m>.
      Nothing else is done.
    </p>

    <p>
      The left side of the replacement in <c>expr1 =&gt; expr2</c> does not need to be a simple variable.
      For example,
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        substitute(sin(x)+ y^2, sin(x) =&gt; exp(t))
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      returns <m>e^t+y^2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If we want to evaluate the expression <c>p</c> when <c>x=1/2</c>, then
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        p1 = substitute(p, x=&gt; 1//2)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      returns <m>-1/8</m>.
      Note that I used the rational number <c>1//2</c> to get an exact result.
      If I had used <c>1/2</c>, then using floating-point division would result in a floating point number.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-symbolic-expanding-simplifying">
    <title>Expanding and Simplifying Symbolic Expression</title>

    <p>
      One of the nice features of a computer algebra system is to feed a complicated expression and the result is
      simplified to a much nicer one.
      This without the tedium of doing it by hand.
      This section examines how to both expand expressions and simplify as well.
    </p>

    <p>
      Some expressions are automatically simplified.
      If you enter
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        4x - 3x^2 + (7x-2x^2)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      julia returns <m>11x-5x^2</m>, therefore similar terms are collected as is <m>(x^2)^3</m> rewritten as <m>x^6</m>.
      Some expressions are not automatically simplified.
      For example, if we have <c>sin(x)^2+cos(x)^2</c>, it would be nice for this to be simplified to 1, but if we
      enter:
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        sin(x)^2+cos(x)^2
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      then it is returns as such.
      We can replace this with 1 by performing:
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        simplify(sin(x)^2+cos(x)^2)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      then the result is <m>1</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Simplifying is often used in conjunction with the <c>expand</c> function, which is used mainly for applying the
      distributive law over polynomials.
      For example:
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        expand((x+y)^3)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      which returns <m>x^{3} + 3 x^{2} y + 3 y^{2}x + y^{3}</m>.
      We can restore the factored form
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-adv-symbolic-variables">
    <title>A Deeper Dive into Symbolic Variables</title>

    <p>
      Above, we did some basics of symbolic variables.
      However, there is a lot more that we didn't discuss above.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here, <c>p</c> is a symbolic expression representing the polynomial <c>3x^3 - x</c>.
      We can check the type of our symbolic variable and expression using the <c>typeof</c> function:
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        typeof(x)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      This will return <c>Num</c>, indicating that <c>x</c> is a symbolic variable.
      Similarly, we can check the type of our symbolic expression:
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        typeof(p)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      This will also return <c>Num</c>, indicating that <c>p</c> is a symbolic expression.
      With symbolic variables and expressions defined, we are now ready to perform various symbolic calculations, such
      as simplification, differentiation, and integration, which we will explore in the following sections.
    </p>

    <p>
      As in mathematics, we often wish to write a variable with a subscript indicating a group of variables should be
      considered as a set of variables.
      If we define
    </p>

    <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
      <code>
        @variables a[1:4]
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      then we have made the variables <c>a[1], a[2], a[3]</c> and <c>a[4]</c>.
      Entering <c>typeof(a)</c> returns <c>Symbolics.Arr{Num, 1}</c>, which takes a little unpacking.
      Notice that it is not a Vector of variables (of type <c>Num</c> ), which might be expected.
      The Symbolics package has a array-like structure called <c>Arr</c> to mimic the array, however it is used to hold
      symbolic variables and expressions.
    </p>

    <p>
      Throughout this chapter, we have seen the <c>Num</c> datatype.
      Again, this indicates that the variables or expression is symbolic, which distinguishes it from the <c>Number</c>
      abstract data type.
      In many cases, defining the variable with no additional information, but if we want to ensure that a variable is a
      Real or a Complex variable, the we can add the extra typing information like:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        @variables z::Complex q::Real
      </code>
    </program>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Handling <m>\pi</m> and <m>e</m> Symbolically</title>

      <p>
        When working with mathematics, we run into <m>\pi</m> and <m>e</m> all over the place and if we want to do things symbolically, we will want to do the same in Julia.  If we just add <m>\pi</m> to an expression like<c>π * x</c> or <c>pi * x</c>, then the ouptut is as expected as <m>\pi x</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        However, if we enter <c>pi/2 *x</c>, then floating point division is performed on <c>π</c> and this is returned as <c>1.5708 x</c>.   
      </p>
      <p>
        To keep everything symbolic, we can do as we did for <m>\sqrt{2}</m> and wrap the <m>\pi</m> in a <c>Num</c> and enter this as <c>Num(pi)/2*x</c> which returns it as 
        <md>
          \frac{1}{2} \pi x
        </md>
        which is more like we expect.<fn>Personally, I would like this written as <m>\frac{\pi}{2} x</m> but I'll work on my own personal issue and expectations.</fn> If you enter <c>Num(pi)</c> often, you can always assign it a variable, like <c>PI = Num(pi)</c> and then use <c>PI</c> in your expressions.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>
</chapter>